From fe8b3019329c6f425a6170dc45d6ff0271571e3c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Michael John <mj@tierone.software>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 22:33:29 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] libfetch: reject unrepresentable Content-Length/Content-Range
 values

http_parse_length() (http.c:921) and http_parse_range() (:945/:949/
:954) accumulated attacker-controlled decimal header values with
plain off_t multiply-and-add, and the range check `len < last - first
+ 1` plus `*length = last - first + 1` (:955/:963) overflowed for a
range ending at OFF_MAX -- UBSan signed-integer overflow throughout,
new versus the P2 "1M execs clean" baseline.

Beyond the UB this is an integrity bug: the wrapped value becomes
fetch(1)'s believed body length.  Content-Length: 18446744073709551621
(2^64+5) wrapped to 5 and fetch silently accepted a 5-byte truncation
of a 10-byte response with exit 0 (a MITM can truncate any download
undetectably), and a 20-digit value wrapped to 8219922592958115050
made fetch report a complete transfer as truncated.  PoC-verified
pre-fix and post-fix against a local server.

Accumulate through http_accum_digit() (rejecting anything past OFF_MAX,
the dd/grep OFF_MAX fallback idiom) and compare the range without the
+1: surviving the check implies last - first + 1 <= len <= OFF_MAX, so
the later +1 cannot overflow.  Unrepresentable values now leave the
length unknown (-1) and fetch falls back to read-to-EOF instead of
trusting a wrapped length.  Pinned by the fetch group in
12-regress-tools.sh; the overflow inputs are kept as fuzz/corpus/http/
seeds (overflow-*, wrap-*, reply-*).

Co-Authored-By: Hermes Agent <hermes@tierone.software>
